WASHINGTON, DC – Today, Congresswoman April McClain Delaney (MD-06) marks one month since she was sworn into office to serve Maryland’s 6th District in Congress. Congresswoman McClain Delaney and her team hit the ground running, quickly opening her district office in downtown Frederick just two weeks into her term.

“My number one priority is listening to our district’s concerns and bringing them to Washington. As constituents navigate Social Security, Veterans Affairs and numerous other federal agencies, my office is here to help deliver the resources they deserve,” said Rep. McClain Delaney. “I am proud of what we’ve accomplished over the last month, but there is so much more work to do, and I will never stop fighting for Maryland’s 6th District in Congress.”

Over the last month, the Congresswoman’s team has recovered $108,400 in savings for constituents over the next 10 years. The team has also answered over 1,000 calls and emails this month alone and opened 563 cases to help constituents with federal agencies and other needs.

In January, she was selected to serve on the House Agriculture Committee and its “Conservation, Research, and Biotechnology” and “Commodity Markets, Digital Assets, and Rural Development” subcommittees to represent Maryland’s farmers and agricultural interests.

She was also selected to serve on the Science, Space and Technology Committee to invest in our state and nation’s innovation hubs and empower our engineers, scientists and changemakers to build a brighter, safer and more inclusive future.

To amplify the voices of her district, she has also joined the:

Problem Solvers Caucus to work with a bipartisan group of lawmakers split evenly between Democrats and Republicans to develop legislative solutions to the country’s most pressing problems;

New Dems Coalition to join pragmatic, pro-business Democrats focused on working across the aisle;

Democratic Women’s Caucus to further women’s freedoms;

Reproductive Freedom Caucus to defend a woman’s right to make her own healthcare decisions;

AI Caucus to boost technological innovation while protecting our communities’ privacy;

Labor Caucus to support America’s workers;

Congressional Equality Caucus to ensure every American is treated fairly under the law; and,

Sustainable Energy & Environment Coalition to ensure a safe and healthy environment for our children and grandchildren.

For updates, follow Rep. McClain Delaney at @RepAprilDelaney.

Congresswoman April McClain Delaney was elected in 2024 to serve Maryland’s 6th Congressional District, which encompasses all or part of Montgomery, Frederick, Washington, Allegany and Garrett Counties. In Congress, McClain Delaney is focused on improving US competitiveness and innovation, protecting our natural environment and championing policies that represent the values and priorities of Maryland’s 6th District.

(Washington, DC) — In his first speech on the floor of the U.S. House of Representatives, Congressman Johnny Olszewski (MD-02) today announced his support for a legislative suite aimed at lowering and offsetting costs for Americans and called out Republican leadership for refusing to allow votes on policies to help struggling families more than one month into the session.

Congressman Olszewski introduced a tracker he will post on his social media pages everyday until the House votes on a bill to lower costs.

You can see his speech in its entirety here.

“The American people elected all of us with one clear mandate: to lower costs,” Congressman Olszewski said. “We are now 32 days into this session of Congress. Sadly, we have not held a single vote – not even a committee hearing – on legislation to lower costs for our constituents who are struggling at the grocery store and the gas station.

While we waste time on partisan bills that do nothing to lower costs, Americans continue to struggle.”

Congressman Olszewski announced his cosponsorship of several bills to address the rising costs for families including:

The American Family Act, which provides for a new, fully-refundable child tax credit and monthly advance payments of credit amounts;

The Earned Income Tax Credit Modernization Act, which would extend the EITC to taxpayers with certain dependents, including children and aged dependents, and to qualifying students. It also establishes a matching grant program for tax return preparation assistance for low-income taxpayers;

The Caring for All Families Act, which would expand eligibility for paid family and medical leave and provide additional leave for parents and family caregivers. The bill also grants leave for employees to care for a domestic partner with serious health conditions;

The Expanding Penalty Free Withdrawal Act, which would help unemployed Americans tap into their retirement accounts to stay afloat;

The Young Americans Financial Literacy Act, which would provide grants for financial literacy education programs to help Americans make better-informed financial decisions.

“These are all bills that should be bipartisan — and they should be our focus,” Congressman Olszewski said. “So let’s roll up our sleeves and let’s get to work.”

